# One shared graph. Parallel edges overlay two update mechanisms.
# Each sum is one cached computation. Numeric deltas are used here.
direction: down

classes: {
  source_a: {
    style: {fill: "#FCE7F3"; stroke: "#BE185D"; font-color: "#831843"; border-radius: 10}
  }
  source_b: {
    style: {fill: "#DBEAFE"; stroke: "#1D4ED8"; font-color: "#1E3A8A"; border-radius: 10}
  }
  source_c: {
    style: {fill: "#EDE9FE"; stroke: "#7C3AED"; font-color: "#4C1D95"; border-radius: 10}
  }
  updated_node: {
    style: {fill: "#FFF7ED"; stroke: "#C2410C"; font-color: "#7C2D12"; border-radius: 10}
  }
  stable_node: {
    style: {fill: "#DCFCE7"; stroke: "#15803D"; font-color: "#14532D"; border-radius: 10}
  }
  invalidation_edge: {
    style: {stroke: "#C2410C"; font-color: "#C2410C"; stroke-width: 3; stroke-dash: 5}
  }
  delta_edge: {
    style: {stroke: "#2563EB"; font-color: "#1D4ED8"; stroke-width: 3}
  }
  quiet_edge: {
    style: {stroke: "#15803D"; font-color: "#15803D"; stroke-dash: 2}
  }
}

shared_graph: "ONE GRAPH · A changes 1 → 5 · B and C stay fixed" {
  direction: down
  style.fill: transparent
  source_group: "INPUTS" {
    style.fill: transparent
    value_a: "A: 1 → 5\nΔA = +4" {
      class: source_a
      style.double-border: true
    }
    value_b: "B = 2\nΔB = 0" {class: source_b}
    value_c: "C = 3\nΔC = 0" {class: source_c}
  }
  sum_ab: "L = A + B\n3 → 7" {class: updated_node}
  sum_bc: "R = B + C\n5 → 5" {class: stable_node}
  sum_total: "O = L + R\n8 → 12" {
    class: updated_node
    style.double-border: true
  }

  # Repeated endpoints are intentional: D2 parallel edges.
  source_group.value_a -> sum_ab: "e1 · possibly stale" {class: invalidation_edge}
  source_group.value_a -> sum_ab: "e1 · ΔA = +4" {class: delta_edge}
  source_group.value_b -> sum_ab: "e2 · reuse B / ΔB = 0" {class: quiet_edge}
  source_group.value_b -> sum_bc: "e3 · unchanged / ΔB = 0" {class: quiet_edge}
  source_group.value_c -> sum_bc: "e4 · unchanged / ΔC = 0" {class: quiet_edge}
  sum_ab -> sum_total: "e5 · possibly stale" {class: invalidation_edge}
  sum_ab -> sum_total: "e5 · ΔL = +4" {class: delta_edge}
  sum_bc -> sum_total: "e6 · reuse R / ΔR = 0" {class: quiet_edge}
}

comparison_table: |||md
  ## Read the overlay

  **Orange dashed:** cached dependency may be stale.
  **Blue solid:** explicit numeric change.
  **Green dotted:** unchanged dependency or zero change.
  Parallel edges are two descriptions of the same logical connection.

  ## What happens at each location

  | ID | Salsa / SWI incremental: cached queries | DBSP: numeric-delta circuit |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | A | Store 5; record input change. | Supply ΔA = +4. |
  | B, C | Values and dependencies stay unchanged. | ΔB = 0; ΔC = 0. |
  | e1: A → L | L may be stale because A changed. | Carry +4 into L. |
  | e2: B → L | Read unchanged B = 2 when L reruns. | Contribute 0. |
  | L | On demand, rerun 5 + 2 = 7; compare with cached 3. | ΔL = 4 + 0 = 4; accumulated L becomes 7. |
  | e3: B → R | Dependency unchanged. | Contribute 0. |
  | e4: C → R | Dependency unchanged. | Contribute 0. |
  | R | Reuse cached 5; body need not run. | ΔR = 0; accumulated R stays 5. |
  | e5: L → O | O may be stale; refreshed L = 7 differs from old 3. | Carry +4 into O. |
  | e6: R → O | Read cached R = 5 when O reruns. | Contribute 0. |
  | O | On demand, rerun 7 + 5 = 12. | ΔO = 4 + 0 = 4; accumulate 8 + 4 = 12. |

  ## What the algorithms remember

  | Technique | Tracked information | When this example updates |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Salsa | Query results, observed dependencies, verification/change revisions. | Requested query validates dependencies and reruns affected bodies. |
  | SWI incremental | Tabled answers, dependency graph, table invalidation state. | A requested invalid table triggers reevaluation. |
  | DBSP | Circuit operators and state needed by their incremental formulas. | Circuit processes the input batch. |

  ## Code-shaped difference

  Original: `L = A + B; R = B + C; O = L + R`

  Cached refresh: `newL = newA + B; newO = newL + cachedR`

  Delta computation: `dL = dA + dB; dR = dB + dC; dO = dL + dR`

  Materialized values: `L += dL; R += dR; O += dO`

  ## Scope

  Each sum is one cached computation. A, B and C must be separately tracked
  inputs for the shown cache reuse; coarse shared dependencies can invalidate
  more work. Salsa and SWI have different internal bookkeeping.
  Zero deltas do not guarantee skipped operator scheduling.
  DBSP need not physically materialize every intermediate shown here.
  The drawing compares update mechanisms, not performance.

  ## Sources

  [Salsa](https://salsa-rs.github.io/salsa/reference/algorithm.html)

  [SWI incremental](https://www.swi-prolog.org/pldoc/man?section=tabling-incremental)

  [DBSP](https://www.vldb.org/pvldb/vol16/p1601-budiu.pdf)
|||

shared_graph -> comparison_table: {style.opacity: 0}
